<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isocrates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">436–<a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isocrates was an Athenian orator and teacher of rhetoric. Around 392 BCE he opened a school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> that trained statesmen, historians and orators from across the Greek world. In written speeches such as the 'Panegyricus' and 'To Philip' he urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to unite against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, eventually under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isocrates.jpg" alt="Isocrates" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: shakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isocrates_pushkin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
